{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition triggers the start of a specification document review — for example, a new document version being published, a change request being approved, or a scheduled review date?",
      "answer": "The review begins when a new version of the specification becomes available alongside a corresponding list of feature modifications. The reviewer first gathers both the updated document and the associated change inventory before any review work starts. No other specific triggering conditions—such as scheduled dates or formal approval gates—are defined for initiating the process."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "How does the engineer determine which parts of the document to review — do they use a checklist, compare against a previous version using a diff, or read the entire document from scratch?",
      "answer": "The engineer cross-references the list of feature modifications against the document's content to identify gaps, then separately checks the text for consistent terminology and proper formatting. A structured checklist guides both steps. This means coverage verification and editorial quality checks are treated as two distinct but sequential passes rather than a single read-through."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "When an issue is found, how is it categorized or prioritized — is there a defined classification scheme such as severity levels or issue types, and does the category affect how the issue is handled next?",
      "answer": "The reference workflow indicates that comments can be grouped by section, severity, and responsible party, suggesting some classification scheme exists. However, the specific severity levels or issue types are not defined in detail. What is clear is that grouping affects how comments are organized and communicated, but the exact downstream handling rules per category are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "Where and how are review findings recorded and communicated to the relevant parties — for example, inline document comments, a separate issue tracker, a structured review form, or email?",
      "answer": "Findings are captured using a dedicated commenting tool and logged as a structured list of requested changes. These records are then handed off to the person who authored the document so corrections can be made. A separate tracking system for change requests is also part of the environment, suggesting findings feed into a formal change log alongside inline annotations."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Besides the reviewing engineer, who else participates in the review process, and who is responsible for resolving the issues that are identified?",
      "answer": "Three other parties are involved: the person who originally wrote the document handles corrections; the individual responsible for the feature confirms that revisions are accurate; and a designated authority gives final sign-off. The document author resolves the issues, but the feature owner must validate that the changes correctly reflect the intended functionality before approval is sought."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What criteria must be met for a review to be considered complete — is a formal sign-off required, and is a follow-up review conducted after corrections are made?",
      "answer": "Completion requires that all feature modifications are properly reflected in the document and that the revised content is deemed acceptable. A formal approval from a designated authority is mandatory. After the author submits corrections, the feature owner validates them first; if the revisions are unsatisfactory, another round of corrections is requested before the approver can give final sign-off."
    }
  ]
}
